
<p class="wp-block-paragraph">I&#8217;ve mentioned a few times in the MacSparky Labs that I pay for web search. That always raises eyebrows. So I thought I&#8217;d explain.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Even Google now injects AI slop before giving you search results, and there&#8217;s a good argument that search is going the way of the dodo. I use AI often, and yet I still find use for search.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">When researching, I often don&#8217;t want an AI synthesis (or I just don&#8217;t want to burn unnecessary and expensive tokens). Search is very much a tool I still want on my belt.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Another argument is that search isn&#8217;t useful anymore. That one&#8217;s true. Type a question into Google and count how far you scroll before you hit something a human wrote to be useful rather than to rank. Ads, then a shopping carousel, then four pages of SEO sludge, then maybe a real result. It got worse slowly enough that most people stopped noticing. Advertisers pay the bills, and as a result most search is garbage.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">So I&nbsp;<a href="https://kagi.com/">pay Kagi for search</a>. With Kagi, nobody can buy their way onto my results page because there&#8217;s nowhere to buy. The company sells subscriptions and that&#8217;s the whole plan. No ads, no tracker network, just search results.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The pricing is simple. Five dollars a month gets you 300 searches. Ten gets you unlimited, which is what I&#8217;m on. Twenty-five adds the premium AI models if you want them. The trial gives you 100 free searches, which is plenty to know. If you&#8217;d like to hear the full backstory,&nbsp;<a href="https://www.relay.fm/mpu/809">we interviewed the Kagi CEO on Mac Power Users last year</a>.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">What you get is a results page that&#8217;s just results. You also have control over the way it ranks for you. You can raise a domain or lower it. I&#8217;ve blocked a handful of content farms that used to clog technical searches. I&#8217;ve pinned a few sites I trust. It feels like my own bespoke search now.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">It isn&#8217;t perfect. Local results are the weak spot, so about once a week I type !g in front of a query and Kagi hands it off to Google. (They call those Bangs.) I mostly use it to find out whether a restaurant is open.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">I&#8217;ve spent money on plenty of dumb things in my life. I&#8217;ve got a drawer full of cables for hardware I no longer own.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Kagi is not one of those mistakes. It&#8217;s ten dollars for a tool I touch many times a day. And every time it does the job without trying to sell me a mattress.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">My money is on more of us doing this. As more of the internet gets&nbsp;<em>generated</em>&nbsp;instead of written, the tools worth having will be the ones with nothing to sell you.</p>



